OSAL / CMN / BE14 datapath review (2026-11-05)

Baseline: ImmortalWrt 25.12 d7b29bda, Linux 6.12.112, GCC 14.3.0.
The BPI-R4 profile is resolved from BananaPi-BPI-R4 0ee0faf: OSAL enabled,
WiFi7/OSAL cfg80211 disabled, MTK vendor WEXT enabled, MT7990 + MT7991.
The changes keep this configuration.

Actual selected BE14 path

The selected mt_wifi module uses its own Linux netdevice and SKB adapter.
Its netdevice registration is RtmpOSNetDevAttach in mt_wifi/os/linux/rt_linux.c.
Its transmit entry is rt28xx_send_packets -> RTMPSendPackets, followed by the
WiFi transmit processing and hwifi_tx_data/hwifi_tx_mgmt -> HWIFI tx_queue.
HWIFI obtains tokens, writes descriptors and hands packets to PCI/WED.

On receive, pci_dma_rx_process builds SKBs and carries mtk_bus_rx_info in cb;
mtk_bus_rx_process -> the HWIFI MAC receive implementation -> connac_if_rx ->
mtk_mac_rx_napi enter the selected WiFi adapter. Its Linux receive entry is
RtmpOsPktRcvHandle (napi_gro_receive during a poll, netif_rx otherwise).
The existing software-RX HNAT metadata invalidation patches remain in this chain.

ELF imports, not just Makefile selections, establish the OSAL boundary. The
selected mt_wifi module imports 15 OSAL symbols. Across mt_wifi and the built
HWIFI/MT7990/MT7991/PCI/WED/Connac modules, the union is 22 symbols:

mt_call_rcu, mt_create_workqueue, mt_debugfs_create_devm_seqfile,
mt_debugfs_create_dir, mt_debugfs_create_file, mt_debugfs_lookup,
mt_debugfs_remove_recursive, mt_destroy_workqueue, mt_idr_alloc, mt_idr_find,
mt_idr_remove, mt_init_delayed_work, mt_init_dummy_netdev, mt_ktime_get,
mt_ktime_get_real_ts64, mt_msecs_to_jiffies, mt_queue_delayed_work,
mt_rcu_barrier, mt_rcu_read_lock, mt_rcu_read_unlock, mt_skb_pull_rcsum,
mt_synchronize_rcu.

The newly added mt_skb_pull_rcsum bridge is called by the real BE14 VLAN
receive helper RtmpOsSkbPullRcsum. OSAL and the alternative mt_wifi_cmn provider
both implement the same ABI. The proprietary module no longer imports the
GPL-only skb_pull_rcsum directly. Its WEXT build has no cfg80211 imports.

Actual-driver fixes

RtmpOsSkbPullRcsum previously pulled first and calculated a checksum over the
wrong bytes. ClonePacket overwrote len/tail without preserving nonlinear SKB
invariants. The fixes use the kernel checksum operation and trim a consistent
cloned view, retaining the original fragment ownership. Checksum metadata that
covers the parent view is invalidated on these receive-side slices.

Normal WEXT netdevice teardown now retains the allocated netdev_ops through
NETREG_UNREGISTERING, with an idempotent destructor and explicit final free.
The MAT source-MAC lookup returns NULL on a miss and uses the RCU device list;
the caller keeps its transmit-path RCU protection while using a borrowed result.

hwifi_tx_data previously reached an RCU unlock before acquiring the read lock
when wdev/entry was missing. Management TX also lacked a missing-wdev/fallback-
STA check and a linear-length check for its hardware header. These paths now
balance their locks and reject invalid resources before dereference.

The physical-device queue publishes shutdown under the producer lock before
cancel/destroy. Its timer and atomic producers use that same lock; allocation
failure still leaves the lock/list initialized. MAC deletion is marked queued
only after enqueue succeeds. Task stop treats a nonzero kthread exit code as
completed shutdown, clears the handle, terminates names and checks -EINTR.

HWIFI preserves the negative sign of idr_replace failures and checks a missing
MLD index entry before dereferencing it.

Actual BE14 CMN + cfg80211 alternative

This variant disables the OSAL provider and enables MTK_WIFI7_CFG80211_SUPPORT,
mt_wifi_cmn, HOSTAPD_MBSS_SUPPORT, APCLI_SUPPLICANT_SUPPORT and CFG80211_FULL_OPS.
It uses the SDK's own cfg80211 adapter with mac80211 backports 6.18.39, not the
independent native OSAL adapter below. Its 118 WiFi package Kconfig arguments
are dependency-resolved from the BPI-R4 profile; WNM stays enabled and MAP stays
disabled. The production profile and workflow are unchanged.

Radio allocation, band initialization and netdevice registration failures now
propagate and unwind the resources created by that attempt. AP/STA virtual
interfaces reject exhausted slots and roll back failed wdev initialization,
registration or VIF bookkeeping. Removal explicitly frees child netdevices;
the bootstrap allocation remains owned by HWIFI until final teardown.

Module teardown acquires the wiphy lock after RTNL; cfg80211 callbacks retain
their already-held locks. Device state is checked before unregistering again,
and teardown removes VIF bookkeeping before retiring the device. The unpaired
netdevice-notifier unregister call is removed. Virtual STA creation honors the
request's 4-address setting instead of enabling it merely because it is supported.

Each wiphy now owns its mutable HE/EHT capability data instead of sharing the
physical chip's storage. Initial station capabilities, fixed channel offsets
for single-band radios, 1..4 stream-rate indexing and band-init error handling
are corrected. DFS classification is limited to the 5 GHz band; the SDK's DFS
policy is otherwise retained. The SDK adapter's BE14 MLO support is retained.

Independent native OSAL interfaces

The selected BE14 driver does not call mt_os_net_dev_create/register,
mt_os_pkt_send, or the native mt_cfg80211_register adapter. They remain separate
interfaces and cannot be counted as proof of a complete BE14 native-OSAL port.

Native netdevice fixes cover private-allocation size, name validation, callback
pairing, module references on failed open, stats configuration initialization,
queue bounds, borrowed lookup results and delayed ops release. Native cfg80211
registration errors now propagate to the netdevice caller and unwind only the
wiphy created by that attempt. Radio creation is rejected while the caller
already owns RTNL; virtual interfaces use their existing wiphy. RF-kill/core
callbacks are stopped before backend retirement; channel/rate arrays survive
wiphy_unregister and are freed afterwards.

The channel table now has stable full-band offsets, including a single-band
5/6 GHz radio. Initialization failures and incompatible reinitialization sizes
return failure. Initial station HE/EHT data, 1..4 stream rates and supported
capability declarations are checked. Native add-station and MLO link callbacks
with no backend implementation return -EOPNOTSUPP. Native OSAL does not advertise
MLO or remain-on-channel support without an implementation. This does not change
the selected BE14 WEXT MLO code.

WEXT ESSID replies are checked against both the SSID limit and user capacity.
Native packet fixes cover checked allocation sizes, fragmented slices, unaligned
VLAN writes, RXCSUM feature selection, RCU-safe RPS snapshots/atomic selection,
and backlog dispatch outside softirq. Private WEXT command storage is terminated
and freed using its owned allocation, even if the callback changes its data
pointer. Native vendor replies check the returned length against owned capacity;
event entry points validate their context and include NLA headers/alignment in
their allocation sizes. MLO event flexible-array lengths are checked before use.

The raw void-returning 802.11 conversion/view helpers retain their caller contract:
a linear, writable SKB and sufficient Ethernet/VLAN headroom. Expansion consumes
the old SKB when a replacement is needed, including allocation failure. Receive
handoff consumes the SKB once; a supplied NAPI object belongs to the current poll.
Borrowed device/wiphy pointers require caller-held lifetime/RCU/RTNL protection.
Task initialization/attach/kill and device init/exit are owner-serialized.

Verification and remaining limits

All 25 OSAL, 29 WiFi7, 10 HWIFI and 2 common-provider patches replay with zero
fuzz. The resulting sources are byte-identical to the module build sources.
Both WiFi packages' binary archives contain 107 files and no source overlap.
The prepared AArch64 toolchain/kernel are reused; no full firmware is generated.

Module compile/MODPOST/link checks cover the resolved OSAL+WEXT profile, MT7990,
MT7991, HWIFI, PCI, WED, Connac, the native OSAL optional logging/FIPS combination,
the OSAL cfg80211/backports variant, and the alternative common provider.
The actual BE14 CMN+cfg80211 module and its HWIFI/MT7990/MT7991/PCI/WED/Connac
peers also compile and link with OSAL absent from their symbol inputs. The
prepared kernel retains vendor WEXT support; the alternate WiFi module uses
NO_MTK_WEXT_PRIV and cfg80211 backport headers. This is not a separate build of
a kernel with all WEXT support disabled.
Existing vendor missing-prototype warnings remain; this is not a warning-free
or runtime result.

The optional regression module has nine WEXT cases and a tenth cfg80211 case.
The latter exercises four band masks and two registration-failure stages per
mask using a synthetic backend. These modules compile; no test board has run
them. They do not demonstrate firmware/RF correctness, throughput, upload speed,
station reconnect, hardware offload hits, or FE/SER recovery. The independent
native cfg80211 adapter still needs a matching driver backend and runtime tests;
it is not a drop-in replacement for the selected BE14 WEXT adapter.
